lve the second equation for \\(x - y\\):\n\n\\[x - y = \\frac{1}{4}\\]\n\nNow we have the system:\n\n1. \\(x + y = 4\\)\n2. \\(x - y = \\frac{1}{4}\\)\n\nWe add these two equations to eliminate \\(y\\):\n\n\\[(x + y) + (x - y) = 4 + \\frac{1}{4}\\]\n\\[2x = \\frac{16}{4} + \\frac{1}{4}\\]\n\\[2x = \\frac{17}{4}\\]\n\\[x = \\frac{17}{8}\\]\n\nNext, we substitute \\(x = \\frac{17}{8}\\) back into the first equation to solve for \\(y\\):\n\n\\[\\frac{17}{8} + y = 4\\]\n\\[y = 4 - \\frac{17}{8}\\]\n\\[y = \\frac{32}{8} - \\frac{17}{8}\\]\n\\[y = \\frac{15}{8}\\]\n\nNow we have \\(x = \\frac{17}{8}\\) and \\(y = \\frac{15}{8}\\). We need to find the product \\(xy\\):\n\n\\[xy = \\left(\\frac{17}{8}\\right) \\left(\\frac{15}{8}\\right) = \\frac{17 \\cdot 15}{8 \\cdot 8} = \\frac{255}{64}\\]\n\nTherefore, the value of the product \\(xy\\) is \\(\\boxed{\\frac{255}{64}}\\)."},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{\\frac{255}{64}}","correct":true,"format":"boxed","parseable":true},"id":"algebra:829"}
